#X text 240 83 Attention! The correct order for connecting the strip:
first ground (!) \, then power (+5V) \, then the data pin. This is
necessary to protect the first LED from burning out. It is recommended
to install a 300-500 Ohm resistor in the data circuit.;
#X obj 230 88 cnv 1 3 75 empty empty empty 17 7 0 10 -258049 -1 0;
#X text 240 168 The disconnection procedure is strictly the reverse:
first disconnect the data pin \, then the +5V power \, and finally
ground.;
#X obj 230 173 cnv 1 3 45 empty empty empty 17 7 0 10 -258049 -1 0;
#X text 240 223 When using the object \, you need to specify the SPI
bus number (the @spi_bus property).;
#X obj 230 228 cnv 1 2 30 empty empty empty 17 7 0 10 -260289 -1 0;
#X text 240 263 The object uses an internal pixel buffer \, which you
work with by sending messages to the object. After finishing work with
the pixel buffer \, it needs to be sent to the LED strip using a bang
or the [flush( command.;
#X obj 230 268 cnv 1 2 60 empty empty empty 17 7 0 10 -158925 -1 0;
#X text 240 333 To specify the group of pixels we are going to work
with \, two types of ranges are used: @slice and @lslice.;
#X obj 240 376 ui.link @background_color 0.90196 0.90196 0.90196
@title SPI information @url ceammc_rpi_spi-info.pd;
